Z||000005||Archie||11-01-2000||08:07 AM||||ok, Mario... I am doing the wire harness for a '86 2M4 5-speed V-8 conversion today. I wonder if anyone minds if I take pictures of the harness I'm modifying today and post them up this evening for you?<P>BTW, you will keep the jundtion box and you'll need the 2 vacuum lines if you intend to use the cruse control and have the vapor cannister working. Just hook them up to manifold vacuum.<P>Also, it will be easier if you actually remove the harness from the car to modify it. Just unplug it from the ECM, unplug the 2 (black/clear) wire connectors below the ECM and take the harness out thru the bulkhead toward the engine compartment side. So, if the harness is still in the car take it ut today.<P>Archie<p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Zorba||11-01-2000||11:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>ok, Mario... I am doing the wire harness for a '86 2M4 5-speed V-8 conversion today.  (edited 11-01-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lucky me! Yes. Wiring harness has been removed from the car. Wire cutters in hand.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000007||Archie||11-01-2000||02:26 PM||||Ok, here goes..... I hope nobody gets mad about this post being so long.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-330s.JPG"> <P>Here is what the 86 2M4 harness looks like as it's just been removed from the car. I usually hang the harness from a high place then remove all the "intestine" covers and start "pruning" the tree. This time I'll walk you thru it wire by wire.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-331s.JPG"> <P>Here is the forward section of the harness showing the wires that are on the passenger side of the bulkhead. These wires will not be modified & will be reconnected just as they were stock. You can see the Gray fitting that fastens into the bulkhead where the wires pass thru the bulkhead. Later as we remove un-needed wires we will cut them back to the engine side of this fitting and secure the ends.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-332s.JPG"> <P>Here is the top section of the harness. Starting from the top of this picture we will discuss each of these leads. The first of these leads going off to the left is the 2 Relays that fasten to the bulkhead behind the driver. One of the relays is for the Fuel pump and the other is the A/C relay. These will be left alone except for one modification. On the A/C Relay we will runs a jumper (or an "H" wire)  between the Light Blue and the Gren wires. This will bypass the Fiero ECM so that the A/C will work.<P>The next wire to the left (with the eye on it) is a Ground wire and will be connected to the engine block.<P>The next one to the left is 2 wires going to a plug. This is the Water Temp sender connector that connects to your Fiero W/T gage. There is a Green wire and a Green/Yellow (Green wire with a Yellow stripe). You will use this plug and the original Fiero W/T sensor and mount the sensor to either the intake manifold or the forward Cylinder head (between plugs #1 & #3) on the Chevy engine.<P>The next wire down on the left is the Connector to plug into the backup light switch on the 5 speed transmission. We will retain this item and reconnect it to the switch upon installation.<P>The Top wire on the right is the Purple wire that went to the original Fiero starter solonoid, we will retain this wire and attach it to the small Spade terminal on the new starter.<P>The next wire down on the right is the O2 sensor wire. This will be removed from the harness. It'‘s non functional on a carburated V-8 Fiero and on a Fuel injected application the aftermarket Wire harness will have it's own connector for the O2 sensor.<P>The next 2 wires on the right are Ground wires they will be retained and routed to the same engine ground as the wire above was.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-333s.JPG"> <P>The wires on the top left of this pic. Include another pair of ground wires to connect to Ground as above.<P>And there is the 2 wire plug with Purple/White and Yellow wires, that one connects to the original Fiero speed sender on the transmission. (On a Fuel Injected engine there will be 2 wires from the aftermarket harness that will splice into these wires).<P>The next 2 wire connctor on the left has a Black and a Yellow wire going to it. This originally connected to a Water Temp. Sensor that connected to the Fiero ECM for Cold Start injection. Those wires will be eliminated. (On a Fuel Injected Chevy engine the aftermarket harness will have provisions to replace this function)..<P>Going down further on the left that Green connector has 3 wires going to it. That is for the original Fiero MAP sensor. These wires are removed from the harness. (On a Fuel Injected Chevy engine the aftermarket harness will have provisions to replace this function)..<P>Curled around kind of behind the MAP wires (in this picture) are 2 plugs that originally went to the Distributerless ignition on this engine. One plug has 5 wires going into it, that plug and all it's wires are removed from tis harness for the SBC engine. The other connector has a large Pink Wire and a smaller black wire going to it. We keep these 2 wires, the Pink one goes to the BATT terminal on the SBC distributer and the Black wire (on some Fieros this wire is White) goes to the TACH connector on the SBC distributor.<P>The next wire on the left is the Oil Pressure sender connector. It has 3 wires going to it they are Tan, Tan/White & Orange/Black. We will retain this plug and mount the original Oil Presure sending unit into the new SBC engine. (On a Fuel Injected Chevy engine the aftermarket harness will have provisions for a different type of sending unit you will be using it and will be retaining the Tan wire from this plug and connecting it to the new oil pressure sendor so that your original Oil Pressure gage will work).<P>The bottom group of wires.on the left in this picture are the original wires that connected to the Fiero Alternator. Since we reuse that alternator these wires will be retained and reconnected as they were before.<P>The one pair of wires coming off to the right in tis picture are the Blue & Red wires that originally went to the 4 banger throttle body. They will be removed from this harness.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-334s.JPG"> <P>This picture will finish out our wire harness modifications. The gang of wires to the left are the wires going to the Fiero A/C compressor. Since we are retaining the Fiero A/C system on tis car we will keep these wires and reconnect them as they were originally.<P>From the top right in this picture you have 3 groups of wires that used to go to the Fiero Throttle body on the 4 banger. They include a 3 wire connector for the original IAC and a 2 wire connector that went to the Air Temp. Sensor in the intake manifold near the throttle body & a 4 wire connector that went to the TPS sensor (this is only 3 wires on some Fieros) Since we are no longer using this Throttle body these wires are all removed from the harness on this application.<P>The last fitting in the bottom center of this picture is the connectors that used to connect to the Junction block over on the right side inner fender well just to the rear of the old Battery box. This will reconnect to the same connections as it was before.<P>Additionally there are 3 other wire connections you will need to make.<P>1) You will recall from above that we have several Ground wires connected to the engine block, you will want to connect a heavy ground wire to this same connection on the engine block and route it to connect to a good Chassis Ground.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-335s.JPG"> <P>2 & 3) On the starter the positive battery cable will connect to the empty threaded terminal on the starter solonoid. (The top terminal in this picture) Another heavy wire should connect to the same terminal and be routed to the screw on stud on the back of the alternator. (BTW, in this picture the Purple starter solonoid wire mentioned above connects to the spade terminal on the left.)<P>I think that's it.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Zorba||11-02-2000||01:56 AM|||| Thank you very much. I have installed the wiring as indicated. I must have screwed something up in the junction box. Radio, lights work but no cranking. With the ignition key on, I have no power on either pink or purple wires. The pink wire is going to the BAT terminal on the HEI distributor and the PURPLE think wire is going to the spade connector on the starter. The BATTERY cable is connected to the starter and so is the alternator wire.<BR>Below the junction box, there are two wires connected together with a loop at the end. When I hook those two wires to the battery, the lights and radio work. I figured I would screw something up. My question then is, is the purple wire "hot" with the ignition on as well as the pink one? I assume I don't want a "hot all the time" going to the distributor or the car won't shut off.<BR>I got the water temp, oil sender and tach connected( white wire to tach on hei distributor)...<P>Where did I go wrong?||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||11-02-2000||08:01 AM||||Archie,<P>You are AWESOME! I can't imagine another vendor who would go to so much trouble and then, post this information on an open forum. This goes to show ME just how much customer service means to YOU.<P>How refreashing,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000010||Archie||11-02-2000||08:58 AM||||Mario, I don't know what you did wrong as I'm not there t help you but. The Purple wire should be hot only when the key is in the START position and the Pink Wire should be hot in the START & the RUN position.<P>Thanks Roy.<P>Archie||||reg||1
